-- title:
--   The adic valuation of a place is rank one discrete
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ and $F$ be fields with $F$ a $K$-algebra, and let $v$ be a place of $F$ over $K$ in the sense of the project's structure `Place K F`: a valuation subring $\mathcal{O}_v \subseteq F$ which contains $\operatorname{algebraMap} K F (a)$ for every $a \in K$, which is not all of $F$, and which is a principal ideal ring. Since $\mathcal{O}_v$ is then a discrete valuation ring, its maximal ideal is a point of the height-one spectrum, and `v.adicValuation` is the associated $\mathbb{Z}^{m0}$-valued valuation on $F$ obtained from that height-one prime, i.e. the normalised valuation attached to $v$. The assertion is that this valuation satisfies `Valuation.IsRankOneDiscrete`: it is a rank one discrete valuation, its value group being nontrivial and generated by a single element. The statement is phrased as a theorem about an arbitrary place rather than as an instance, so that it can be introduced locally where needed.
--
--   This records that the normalised valuation of a place of a function field $F/K$ is discrete of rank one, the basic fact underlying the definition of the order function $\operatorname{ord}_v$ and hence of divisors and the divisor class group. It is used in the identification of valuation subrings of a rational function field with the points of the height-one spectrum, namely in [`AlgebraicCurve.RationalFunctionField.toValuationSubring_eq_of_forall_ne_ofHeightOneSpectrum`](thm.html#AlgebraicCurve.RationalFunctionField.toValuationSubring_eq_of_forall_ne_ofHeightOneSpectrum).
